.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
.\" Hand-written (the program has no clap definitions): what the engine
.\" starts it with, not what a user types.
.TH VELARX-DIR 1 "" "velarx" "Velarx"
.SH NAME
velarx\-dir \- the fetcher of a directory of items named by their digests
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B velarx\-dir
.BI \-\-name " NAME"
.BI \-\-dir " PATH"
.BI \-\-varlink\-fd " N"
.BI \-\-config\-fd " N"
.BI \-\-staging\-fd " N"
.RB [ \-\-self\-test ]
.br
.B velarx\-dir \-\-push
.BI \-\-varlink\-fd " N"
.BI \-\-config\-fd " N"
.RB [ \-\-self\-test
.IR SCHEME ]
.br
.B velarx\-dir \-\-origin
.BI \-\-varlink\-fd " N"
.BI \-\-config\-fd " N"
.BI \-\-staging\-fd " N"
.RB [ \-\-self\-test
.IR SCHEME ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B velarx\-dir
serves
.B org.velarx.Fetcher
for a directory that a
.B [[sources.external]]
entry with a
.I dir
names (see
.BR velarx.conf (5)).  It is started by the engine, never by a user, one
process for each connection of the engine, and found next to
.BR velarx (1)
(or where
.I dir_path
of
.BR velarx.conf (5)
says).  It reads the directory, writes the files of a layer beneath the
staging directory it was given and does nothing else: it links no registry
or HTTP code, and confines itself before it serves (Landlock: read the
directory, write the staging; no network, no execution; seccomp), refusing
to run on a kernel that cannot enforce all of it.  It leaves when its
connection is closed.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-name " NAME"
The name of the source.
.TP
.BI \-\-dir " PATH"
The directory served.
.TP
.BI \-\-varlink\-fd " N"
The connection of the engine that started the process.
.TP
.BI \-\-config\-fd " N"
The limits it takes of the configuration (the services, the largest file it writes and what it inflates), as JSON, in the descriptor
.IR N .
.TP
.BI \-\-staging\-fd " N"
The staging directory of the store.
.TP
.B \-\-self\-test
Confine the process, try from inside what the confinement allows and
refuses and print the results as JSON; nothing is served.  With
.B \-\-push
or
.BR \-\-origin ,
it takes the
.I SCHEME
the process is confined for (descriptor 5 is the target).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR velarx (1),
.BR velarx\-registry (1),
.BR velarx.conf (5)
.SH ORIGIN MODE
With
.B \-\-origin
the process serves
.B org.velarx.Fetcher
as the origin of a pull whose source is a path (\fBoci:DIR[:REF]\fR,
\fBoci\-archive:FILE[:REF]\fR, \fBdocker\-archive:FILE[:REF]\fR,
\fBdir:DIR\fR, or \fBarchive:FILE\fR of
.BR "velarx load" ),
of a root file system (\fBrootfs:PATH\fR) or of a Docker daemon
(\fBdocker\-daemon:IMAGE\fR), one process for each pull.  The
.I source
of
.B Open
is the scheme and the selector (never the path), the
.I target
the directory or the file that the client opened.  The process confines
itself when
.B Open
is answered: it reads what it was handed, writes beneath the staging
directory (\fB\-\-staging\-fd\fR), has no network and executes nothing.  Only then
does it read the archive: the tar, its compression, the index and the
manifests of a docker\-archive.  It serves the index of the images the selector
takes (\fBGetFile("")\fR), their manifests and blobs, and
.B Unpack
of every layer.  What it says is a claim: the engine checks the digests of
the manifests and the configurations, and replays the layers against the
diff ids.
A pull from a path is always whole, never lazy: a lazy image has to open its
origin again when a file is first read, and a path cannot be opened again
(the process was given it once, as a descriptor).
.PP
For \fBrootfs:\fR the
.I target
is a directory or a tar.  The files of a directory, read beneath it, are
written as the tar of one layer, which with a configuration that has no time
in it is an image that is the same whenever the directory is the same.  The
files keep the owners,
modes, times and attributes (not the SELinux label) they have, as the
engine's user namespace sees them (a file of an id it does not map is
owned by the overflow id).  A tar, plain or compressed with gzip or zstd,
is the blob of the layer as it is, and its diff id is the digest of what it
inflates to (within the inflate limits of
.BR [pull] ); an empty file is an empty tar, and a tar with a sparse entry is written
again, uncompressed, with the entry whole.  The selector is the algorithm of
every digest of the image (the layer, its diff id, the configuration and the
manifest): \fBrootfs:\fR is sha256, \fBrootfs:sha512\fR is sha512, and any
other is refused.  For
\fBdocker\-daemon:\fR the selector is the image to save and the
.I target
is the connection that the engine made to the socket of
.B [pull] docker_socket
(see
.BR velarx.conf (5)):
the process opens no socket of its own, it asks
.B GET /images/IMAGE/get
on that connection and reads the archive as it reads a
\fBdocker\-archive:\fR,
whose names its images keep (none for an image saved by its ID).
.SH PUSH MODE
With
.B \-\-push
the process serves
.B org.velarx.Pusher
as the origin of a push to a path, one process for each push.  The mode
is the scheme of the destination that
.B Open
names: \fBoci:\fR writes an OCI image layout, \fBdir:\fR a directory of one
manifest and its blobs, \fBoci\-archive:\fR a tar of a layout; any other
scheme is refused.  The
.I target
of
.B Open
is the directory, or the file, that the client opened.  The process confines
itself when
.B Open
is answered (before it, it has no destination and every call but
.B GetInfo
and
.B Open
is refused):
it writes beneath that directory (an archive: to that file) and nowhere else, has no
network and executes nothing.
